Default cache TTL is **604800 seconds (7 days)**, and the default max entries is **5000**, both defined in `proxy-a.mjs`:

```js
const TTL_MS = +(process.env.CACHE_TTL_SEC || 604800) * 1000;
const MAX_ENTRIES = +(process.env.CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES || 5000);
```

Both are overridable via the `CACHE_TTL_SEC` and `CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES` environment variables (also written into the config by `cli.mjs`).

Only upstream responses with HTTP status `200` are written to the cache (`fsp.writeFile(file, buf)` plus the `.json` meta file, and mirrored to any alias keys). For streamed responses (`wantsStream === true`), there's an extra requirement: the raw SSE body text must contain `message_stop` — i.e. the stream must have completed fully — before it's cached. This is captured in the single condition:

```js
const complete = ur.statusCode === 200 && (!wantsStream || text.includes('message_stop'));
if (complete && !bypass) { ...write to cache... }
```

Source: `proxy-a.mjs:292-307`.

When a request coalesces onto an in-flight identical request whose upstream result turns out **not cacheable** (i.e. status wasn't 200), the proxy replays that result to the waiter with header `x-cache: MISS-COALESCED` (as opposed to `HIT-COALESCED` used when the shared result was a cacheable 200).

Source: `proxy-a.mjs:252-254`
```js
if (r && r.status === 200) { serveHit(res, ..., 'HIT-COALESCED', seq); return; }
if (r) { safe(res, () => { res.writeHead(r.status, { ..., 'x-cache': 'MISS-COALESCED' }); res.end(r.buf); }); ... return; }
```
